#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167931} :activity/id #uuid "92b5fdfc-2225-50d5-b6fe-9d76c920db9c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048833 :topic/id #uuid "c46bbf50-5eed-5a5c-9851-dfb4b3e5f4ec" :topic/math-academy-id 3196} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168039 :tutorial/content "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *odd*, then its Fourier series has *sine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier sine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval, say, $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **odd extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/77/776119c1d430a7d50051ae0e00e254f9d22782bc192aa8a0cab9cc227fe460db.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its odd extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f915ed450be1abf672e3fd86b56c02820514a778a4d4d174409f5bd99da6834.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *odd* function, meaning $f(-x)=-f(x)$. Because $f$ is odd, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *sine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is odd and $\\sin(nx)$ is odd for any $n \\geq 1,$ then $f(x)\\sin(nx)$ must be even.\nSo, the sine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formula*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n1\\. Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the odd extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n2\\. Compute $b_n$ from the half-range integral on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n3\\. Write the Fourier sine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e10dc0a-145b-598c-837b-7a6a2f858c85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14652 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1dcd5d80-021e-5ab2-8637-bf6a5a9faf0e" :step/math-academy-id 595576 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167932}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166567 :question/id #uuid "f7af1dba-6300-56ab-93d2-0f3d1b893dad" :question/math-academy-id "e-21809"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379595}] :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "31f7c3df-5d68-5318-8629-44acb9dd6545" :step/math-academy-id 595577 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166568 :question/id #uuid "1ff70a32-336a-5ff2-bfe8-0df06eec1a7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21810"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315083} {: